 90	1. `<n> need attention` for `NeedsHuman` items only
 91	2. `<n> running` for `Running` items only
 92	3. existing `<n> recent`
 93	
 94	Failed and cancelling items remain visible in expanded Activity but do not make
 95	the compact chip claim that they need human input or are still running. The dot
 96	uses `--warn` for human attention, the existing live accent for running, and a
 97	neutral/empty treatment otherwise. No pulse or decorative animation.
 98	The accessible label and one polite live region announce the same factual count.
 99	
100	### Expanded interaction
101	
102	The popover remains one dialog and one material surface. When activity exists,
103	render an `Activity` section between search and session results:
104	
105	- needs-human items first, then failed, cancelling, and running;
106	- one plain summary row per item, with state, session title, and concise detail;
107	- clicking or pressing Enter on a summary toggles its inline disclosure;
108	- the disclosure may show project, tool/request state, and bounded reason;
109	- one ghost `Open session` action focuses the associated session through
110	  `Daemon::switch_session(id, title)` and closes the Island.
111	
112	Attention controls participate in a real Tab focus trap. Arrow-key/
113	`aria-activedescendant` session navigation remains scoped to the search input,
114	so using a disclosure button can never accidentally switch the selected session.
115	Escape still closes exactly one surface and restores the invoking focus.
116	
117	### Explicit Phase 2A deferrals
118	
119	- no approve/deny inside the Island;
120	- no inline reply/resume or stop action;
121	- no local dismiss/read state;
122	- no raw permission arguments;
123	- no browser, CI, GitHub, room, or Council cards;
124	- no claim that the polling projection is durable attention history.
125	
126	Those mutations follow only after the read-only state and ordering prove useful
127	and their daemon authorization contracts are explicit.
128	
129	## Phase 3A — submitter-authorized permission actions
130	
131	The first mutation slice adds Approve/Deny only when this exact Surface owns the
132	turn's decision token. The public permission snapshot never exposes that token,
133	and the Island must not weaken the daemon's authorization boundary.
134	
135	An expanded permission card is actionable only when all are true:
136	
137	- its `permission_id` exists in the focused Surface's live
138	  `pending_permissions` signal;
139	- the permission belongs to the currently focused session;
140	- `active_decision_token` is present for that submitted turn.
141	
142	When actionable, the disclosure shows one compact action row: danger-ghost
143	`Deny` followed by primary `Approve`. Both reuse `Daemon::decide_permission`,
144	disable while deciding, and never add raw arguments to the Island. The redundant
145	`Open session` action is hidden because the owning session is already focused.
146	A background or foreign-surface permission stays read-only and offers only
147	`Open session`; it must never render a button that will predictably fail token
148	verification.
149	
150	`Allow for session`, inline reply, background stop, and dismiss/acknowledge
151	remain deferred. This slice changes no daemon endpoint or token semantics.
152	
153	## Future typed cards — CI, diffs, and todos
154	
155	CI/GitHub and todo disclosures follow the same plain-row grammar, but require
156	new daemon-owned typed payloads rather than parsing logs or inventing local
157	state.
158	
159	A CI summary may show repository, branch, check state, duration, and bounded
160	pass/fail counts. Its disclosure may lazily load changed-file `+/-` summaries,
161	a small bounded diff excerpt, and one `Open full run/diff` action. Never render a
162	whole patch or raw build log inside the Island. Rerun/cancel actions require
163	explicit daemon capabilities and permission policy.
